--------------------------------------------------

nodeSelector is the simplest way in Kubernetes to constrain a pod so that it only runs on nodes with specific labels. It works as a hard requirement, meaning the Kubernetes scheduler will only place the pod on a node that contains every key-value label pair specified in the pod's nodeSelector field.

How nodeSelector Works

Using nodeSelector is a two-step process involving labeling your node and updating your pod configuration.
• Step 1: Label the node: Attach a key-value label to your target node using kubectl label nodes <node-name> <label-key>=<label-value>. For example, running kubectl label nodes worker-1 disktype=ssd marks worker-1 as having SSD storage.
• Step 2: Add nodeSelector to the Pod: Define the nodeSelector field inside the spec section of your Pod manifest. The scheduler checks all nodes and filters out any that do not match disktype: ssd.

Example Pod Manifest

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: nginx-ssd
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx
    image: nginx:latest
  nodeSelector:
    disktype: ssd


Key Characteristics and Limitations

• Hard constraint only: If no nodes in the cluster have the matching label, the pod will stay in a Pending state and will not be scheduled.

"""
• Exact matching (AND logic): If you list multiple labels under nodeSelector, a node must have all of them to be eligible. It does not support "OR" logic or "NOT" operators.
"""

• Built-in node labels: Kubernetes automatically applies standard labels to nodes—such as kubernetes.io/os=linux or topology.kubernetes.io/zone=us-east-1a—which you can use right away without manually labeling nodes.

• When to upgrade to Node Affinity: For complex rules—like preferring a node without strictly requiring it, or matching against a list of possible values—use nodeAffinity instead of nodeSelector.


