what is the labels on node is changed later?

Nothing happens to pods that are already running on the node when you change or remove its labels. nodeSelector is only checked by the Kubernetes scheduler at the exact moment a pod is being scheduled onto a node, so existing pods will continue running undisturbed.

How Label Changes Affect Pods

• Existing running pods: Pods already bound to the node stay right where they are. Removing or changing a node's label will not evict or restart them.

• Newly scheduled pods: Any new pods created after the label change will follow the updated labels. If the node no longer has the required label, new pods with that nodeSelector will not be placed there.

• Recreated or scaled pods: If an existing pod crashes and is recreated by a Deployment, or if you scale up your replicas, the new pod instances will evaluate the node's current labels and will skip that node if the label no longer matches.

How to Move Existing Pods After a Label Change

If you changed a node's label and actually want the old pods to move off that node, you must manually force them to reschedule:
• Delete the individual pods: Run kubectl delete pod <pod-name> so the Deployment or ReplicaSet creates a fresh pod that gets scheduled onto a node with the right label.
• Drain the node: Run kubectl drain <node-name> --ignore-daemonsets to safely evict all workloads from that node at once.
• Use NoExecute taints instead: If you ever need pods to be automatically evicted the instant a node's status changes, use a taint with the NoExecute effect rather than relying on labels and nodeSelector.
